WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.381 --> 00:00:09.016
In this age of COVID, Americans are changing their housing habits.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:14.909
Able to work from home, they are leaving crowded high-cost cities for less pricey pastures.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:19.407
But even there, housing is in short supply, thanks to decades of overregulation. 

00:00:19.673 --> 00:00:25.539
Large-lot zoning makes it impossible to build small houses on small lots that cost less.

00:00:25.952 --> 00:00:30.188
The Biden infrastructure bill includes a $5 billion plan to do something about this. 

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:34.403
Communities that
agree to relax zoning would qualify for federal money.  

00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:38.226
It’s identified the right problem—but the plan  has its own flaws.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.217
It takes something communities should want to do on their own and turns it into something they need to be paid to accept. 

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.042
It won’t likely incentivize the wealthy locales, where housing is most restricted, to participate.  
 

00:00:50.161 --> 00:00:59.280
Even without aid from Washington, there’s a growing “missing middle” housing movement aimed at
persuading localities to relax their zoning straight-jacket. 

00:00:59.371 --> 00:01:06.738
HUD should provide models, not mandates, to help localities change course. Smart communities will see the advantage.

00:01:07.711 --> 00:01:11.906
Should Washington override local zoning Let us know in our poll.

00:01:11.906 --> 00:01:16.677
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds.  

00:01:16.677 --> 00:01:22.553
And be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

